## 1. Introduction
Malignant tumors with an uncontrolled growth rate are a serious threat to human health worldwide [1]. In the battle against tumors, in addition to traditional technologies such as surgery and chemotherapy, several novel therapeutic modalities have been explored, including photodynamic therapy (PDT), photothermal therapies (PTT), chemodynamic therapy, and radiodynamic therapy [2,3]. In particular, as a noninvasive therapeutic modality, PDT utilizes photosensitizers to generate excessive reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as singlet oxygen $^10_{2}$ , to induce the apoptosis and necrosis of cancer cells upon irradiation with light at wavelengths ranging from the visible to near-infrared region [4,5]. Light-excited PDT takes advantage of its excellent safety and high efficiency, while it also suffers from some limitations, such as the low tissue-penetration depth of light due to the rapid attenuation of optical energy in the tissue. To address the limitation of tissue penetration depth, sonodynamic therapy (SDT) has been developed by using ultrasound (US) as the excitation source, which features high biosafety and a tissue penetration ability of more than $10\mathrm{cm}$ [6–8]. There are two main mechanisms of the sonodynamic effect: cavitation and sonoluminescence [9]. When US interacts with the aqueous environment, it causes a phenomenon known as ‘‘cavitation’’, which involves the nucleation, growth, and impulsive collapse of gas bubbles, thus transferring high heat and pressure to the surrounding microenvironment. Sonoluminescence means the production of light during the irradiation of a solution with US, which can excite the sonosensitizer to generate electron-hole $(\mathrm{e^{-}\mathrm{h^{+}}})$ pairs with the subsequent generation of ROS. Under the irradiation of US, the sonosensitizers can be excited to produce ROS to destroy tumor cells, whereas the ROS generation efficiency of SDT is commonly low in comparison to that of PDT. Taking the tissue penetration depth and ROS production efficiency into consideration, the combination of PDT and SDT can make up for the disadvantages in each mode, thus achieving a synergistic effect on tumor treatment. The key to PDT-SDT synergistic cancer therapy is to develop agents with a photo-sono response. Fortunately, most photosensitizers are also sonosentizers, and the striking examples of photo-sono sensitizers include inorganic semiconductors (such as titania-based nanocomposites [10]) and organic dyes (azo compounds [11], IR780 dyes [12], and porphyrins [13–15], etc.). Compared to these inorganic semiconductors, organic dyes have received much attention due to their excellent biocompatibility and high photo-sono sensitivity. It should be noted that the low solubility, rapid metabolism, and weak tumor-homing ability of these organic dyes in vivo usually cause an unsatisfactory therapeutic effect. Therefore, it is highly necessary to modify these organic photo-sono sensitizers into new agents to achieve high photo-sono therapeutic effects on malignant tumors.
Apart from chemical conjugation and physical absorption, these organic sensitizers can also be assembled with metal ions/clusters to form nanoscale metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [16,17]. MOFs have become a research hotspot in cancer imaging and therapy in recent years due to a surge of advantages, such as high porosity, good hydrophilicity, biodegradability and easy functionalization [18]. In the field of PDT and/or SDT, various kinds of MOFs have been designed and constructed through a self-assembly method. For example, ultrathin Cu-TCPP (TCPP: Tetrakis (4- carboxyphenyl) porphyrin) MOFs were prepared through a facile solvothermal route, and they generated ${}^{1}0_{2}$ under irradiation with $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light and achieved synergistic cancer phototherapy [19]. The $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ -based MOFs were prepared by using the meso-tetrakis (4-sulfonatophenyl) porphyrin as the organic linker via a onestep coordination synthesis, which exhibited efficient $^10_{2}$ generation for destroying cancer cells in vitro and in vivo upon US exposure [6]. In addition, our groups have also designed and synthesized MOFs by assembling $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{3+}$ ions with hematoporphrin, a novel second-generation porphyrin-related sonosensitizer, for tumor PDT and SDT [20,21]. It should be noted that most MOFs were constructed by using heavy metal ions (metals with an atomic number greater than 20 or an element density greater than $5\mathrm{gcm}^{-3}$ [22]) as metal nodes. On the one hand, these heavy metal ions endow MOFs with an additional imaging ability (such as the magnetic resonance imaging ability of $\mathrm{Mn^{3+}/G d^{3+}}$ ions) and therapeutic effects, including glutathione consumption or Fenton/ Fenton-like reactions [21,23]. On the other hand, these heavy metal ions can also bring about some safety concerns, as they have paramagnetism/ferromagnetism or unsaturated 3d orbitals, which would cause significant energy transfer from ligands to metal ions, resulting in a reduction in photo-sono sensitization. For example, the fluorescence and ROS generation ability of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{3+}$ -based MOFs were significantly quenched in comparison to free porphyrin [20]. To improve ROS generation, sub5 nm Gd-hematoporphrin dots were prepared, and they generated a higher concentration of $^10_{2}$ than their aggregated counterparts, whereas they still suffer from serious energy transfer [23]. Therefore, it is still a major challenge to avoid the quenching effect for MOFs.
In contrast to heavy metal ions as metal nodes in MOFs, light metal ions (such as $A|^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ ions) can also be applied as metal nodes for constructing MOFs. Among the MOFs with light metal ions, $A1^{3+}.$ -based MOFs have received increasing interest and are applied in different fields due to the unique octahedral nature of the aluminum center and strong Al-O bonds. For example, MIL-96(Al) MOFs with high thermal stability were used for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ selective catalytic reduction [24], and Al-MOFs were developed with high performance for detecting the tetracycline antibiotic [25]. In the food industry and medicine, Al-based materials have also been used; for example, aluminum hydroxide gel is used as a clinical medicine to neutralize gastric acid and treat ulcers [26], even though some results have proven that a high concentration of ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions can negatively affect neurological outcomes [27]. In addition, the ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions exhibit the electron configuration of $3\ s^{2}$ $3\mathsf{p}^{1}$ , which cannot undergo the low-energy $d\ –d$ transition. To date, only a few $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ -based MOFs with PDT or SDT have been prepared, and they show low cytotoxicity for mice [28,29]. Herein, we have prepared nanoscale hematoporphrin-based frameworks with $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions as metal nodes (AlHFs) via covalent coordination for enhanced photo-sono therapy of malignant tumors. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report for AlHFs regarding both synthesis and PDT-SDT application. The AlHFs were surface-modified with 1,2- distearoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine-N-(polyethylene glycol) (DSPE-PEG) molecules to obtain AlHFs-PEG with high hydrophilicity. Under excitation with $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light or US, AlHFsPEG can produce 3.6-fold or 2.8-fold more $^10_{2}$ species than PEGcoated Fe-hematoporphrin frameworks (FeHFs-PEG) because $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions without 3d orbitals are not beneficial for energy transfer. The AlHFs-PEG exhibited high biocompatibility and could be taken up by cells to produce intracellular $^10_{2}$ to destroy cancer cells. With high biosafety and enhanced photo-sono sensitization, the growth rate of tumors with AlHFs-PEG injection is significantly inhibited upon exposure to both light and US, showing a higher tumor therapeutic efficacy than photodynamic therapy or sonodynamic therapy alone.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
${\sf A l}({\sf N O}_{3})_{3}.9{\sf H}_{2}0$ $(\geq98\%)$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $\scriptstyle\left(\geq99\%\right)$ , ethanol $(\geq99.7\%)$ , methanol $(\geq99.5\%)$ , trimethylamine $(\geq99\%)$ , N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ , and 1,3-diphenyl isobenzofuran (DPBF, $97\%$ were purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. Hematoporphyrin (hematoporphyrin monomethyl ether or HMME, $95\%$ was obtained from Shanghai Xianhui Pharmaceutical Co. Ltd. 1,2- Distearoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine-N-(polyethylene glycol) (DSPE-PEG, $95\%$ ) with an average weight of 5000 was acquired from Shanghai Yanyi Biotechnology Corporation. Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), 40,6-diamidino-2-phenylindole (DAPI), Calcein-AM and propidium iodide (PI) staining kits, $^{2^{\prime},7^{\prime}}$ -dichloro fluorescin diacetate (DCFH-DA), 3-(4,5)-dimethylthiahiazo(-z-y1)- 3,5-di-phenytetrazoliumromide (MTT), and phosphate buffered saline (PBS) were purchased from Beyotime Biotechnology. The catalog numbers of each regent were shown in Table S1.
## 2.2. Synthesis of AlHFs-PEG
The AlHFs were prepared by the assembly of ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions and HMME molecules. Typically, ${\sf A l}({\sf N O}_{3})_{3}.9{\sf H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{30~mg},$ and HMME $(10~\mathrm{mg})$ were dissolved in a solution consisting of $1~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol, and $5\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. Then, the solution was stirred for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ in the dark, followed by the addition of $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ triethylamine and another $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ of stirring at $60^{\circ}C$ . The solution was centrifuged (10000 rpm, 15 min) and washed with a solution (water/ ethanol $=1{:}1{\mathrm{~mL}}$ ) three times to obtain the AlHFs. To improve the colloidal stability, the AlHFs were modified with DSPE-PEG. DSPEPEG $(20~\mathrm{mg})$ was added to an aqueous solution containing AlHFs (5 mL, $1\ \mathrm{mg\mL}^{-1}$ ) and stirred for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The mixture was treated with three cycles of centrifugation $(10000\mathrm{rpm},15\mathrm{min})$ and washing, finally obtaining AlHFs-PEG. The overall concentration of the AlHFs-PEG dispersion was determined by drying a certain volume of concentrated dispersion in a vacuum and then weighing the final powder, and three samples were executed.
## 2.3. Synthesis of FeHFs-PEG
The FeHFs were prepared according to a previous method with some modifications [30]. In brief, 5 mg HMME and $10{\mathrm{~mg~FeCl}}_{3}$ were dissolved in a $10\ \mathrm{mL}$ solution of methanol/triethylamine $(85/15~\mathrm{vol\%})$ under magnetic stirring for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ in the dark. The mixture was sonicated by using an ultrasonic cleaning instrument $(40\mathrm{kHz})$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and the FeHFs were then collected by centrifugation (10000 rpm, $15\mathrm{min}$ ) and washed with a solution (water/etha $\mathrm{nol}=1\mathrm{:}1\ \mathrm{mL}$ ) three times. The FeHFs-PEG were also prepared by mixing FeHFs (5 mL, $1{\mathrm{~mg~mL}}^{-1}$ ) and DSPE-PEG $\mathrm{20~mg)}$ in deionized water under magnetic stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ before centrifugation (10000 rpm, $15\mathrm{min}$ ) and washing.
## 2.4. Characterization
Morphology and composition of samples were characterized by using high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM, FEI Talos F200S) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Escalab 250Xi). Hydrodynamic size and zeta potential were carried out on a Zatasizer Nano Z (Malvern Instruments). The photoabsorption and fluorescence were respectively recorded by using UV–vis spectrometer (Shimadzu UV-3600) and fluorescence spectrometer (FP6600). The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum was acquired from samples in KBr pellets using an IRPrestige-21 spectrometer. The $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light was emitted from a $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ semiconductor laser purchased from China Changchun New Industry Optoelectronics Technology Co., Ltd. The ultrasound probe (WED100) was bought from Shenzhen Weld Medical Electronics Co., Ltd.
## 2.5. $^1{\cal O}_{2}$ generation and detection upon light and US stimulation
The ${}^{1}0_{2}$ probe solution was prepared by dissolving $10~\mathrm{mg}$ DPBF in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. The DPBF solution $(20~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was added into a DMF solution of AlHFs-PEG $2~\mathrm{mL}$ , $30~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ ) and bubbled with airflow, forming a uniform solution. To detect the photodynamic effect of AlHFs-PEG, the mixture was illuminated by a $660\mathrm{nm}$ light at $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ , and the photoabsorption spectra were recorded every $20~s$ by a UV–Vis spectrometer. To detect the sonodynamic effect of AlHFs-PEG, the mixture was exposed to US irradiation $(1.0~\mathrm{MHz},1.75~\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and the photoabsorption was monitored every 3 min by a UV–Vis spectrometer. As a comparison, the $^10_{2}$ generation ability of FeHFs-PEG was also evaluated under the irradiation of $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light and/or the US.
## 2.6. Cytotoxicity of AlHFs-PEG
Mice colorectal cancer (CT26) cells and human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVEC) were cultured with the RPMI-1640 medium containing $10\%$ FBS and $1\%$ penicillin–streptomycin under the standard conditions. CT26 cells were seeded into 96-well plates at the density $10^{4}$ /well and cultured for 12 and $24\mathrm{h}$ , followed by the addition of AlHFs-PEG at a series of concentrations (0, 6, 12, 25, 50, and $75\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ), respectively. After the incubation, cells were washed with PBS twice and incubated with a new medium containing MTT assay for another $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Then the supernatant was abandoned, and $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMSO was added into each well to dissolve the Formazan crystal. The absorbance of each well was read at $490\mathrm{nm}$ by a microplate reader. Five parallels were carried out independently. The cell viabilities were calculated by using the equation $\mathrm{(OD_{samples})/O D_{c o n t r o l})\times100\%}$
## 2.7. Endocytosis and $^1{\cal O}_{2}$ generation within cells
To investigate the endocytosis of AlHFs-PEG, CT26 cells were seeded and cultured in 12-well plates at a density of $2.0\times10^{5},$ /well for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and then AlHFs-PEG at $50~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ was added to each well. After being cultured for 1, 2, 4, and $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ the cells were washed with PBS and imaged by a digital microscope. To study the intracellular $^10_{2}$ generated by AlHFs-PEG, the cells with AlHFs-PEG at $50~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ were cultured for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then washed twice with PBS. Subsequently, a new medium containing DCFH-DA $(20~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ was added to each well for co-culture for another $30\mathrm{min}$ . The cells were treated with $660\mathrm{nm}$ light at $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ (AlHFsPEG $^+$ Light group), US at $1.75\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ (AlHFs-PEG $^+$ US group) or both (AlHFs-PEG $^+$ Light $^+$ US group). For comparison, cells without AlHFs-PEG were also illuminated by light (Light group) or US (US group) under the other identical conditions. After the treatment, the cells were stained with DAPI for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ before imaging.
## 2.8. PDT and SDT of cells by AlHFs-PEG
CT26 cells were seeded in 96-well plates at a density of $1.0\times10^{5}\$ /well for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , followed by the addition of AlHFs-PEG at
$50\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . After incubation for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the cells were illuminated by $660\ \mathrm{nm}$ light at an intensity of $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ for different durations (0, 2, 4, 6, 8, and $10\ \mathrm{min}$ ), US at 1.75 W $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for 0, 3, 6, 9, 12, or $15\mathrm{min}$ or both light and US for 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8 min. For comparison, the cells without AlHFs-PEG were also treated with light and/or US under identical conditions. After different treatments, the cells were washed and further incubated with a new medium containing MTT assay for the viability test. The experiments were independently performed four times. In addition, cells in parallel were costained with the Calcine $\mathsf{A M/P I}$ assay after the treatments and observed under a fluorescence microscope.
## 2.9. Blood analysis
All animal investigations were approved by the Animal Welfare and Research Ethics Committee of Donghua University. To investigate hemolysis, fresh red blood cells (RBCs) were obtained from blood samples of healthy BALB/c mice and purified by washing with PBS and centrifuging (3000 rpm for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) for five cycles. Then, $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of the diluted RBC suspension was added to $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of AlHFs-PEG in PBS at different concentrations (5, 10, 20, 40, 80, and $120\upmu\mathrm{g}\:\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ ). The mixtures were left at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ and then centrifuged at 12000 rpm for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . The absorbance (A) of the supernatant was recorded at $540~\mathrm{nm}$ . In addition, RBCs in deionized water $(+)$ and PBS $(-)$ were used as positive and negative controls, respectively. The percent hemolysis of the RBCs was calculated using the following formula: (Asample -A negative)/(Apositive $-\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{negative}})\ \times\ 100\%$ . To analyze the blood biochemistry, healthy BALB/c mice were intravenously injected with AlHFs-PEG $(5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}).$ ) and then sacrificed on the 7th and 28th days after injection. Another healthy $\mathsf{B A L B}/\mathsf{c}$ mice were used as the control. Before the mice were euthanized, fresh blood samples were collected for biochemistry analysis.
## 2.10. PDT-SDT of tumor models and histological examinatio
The tumor models of mice were established by subcutaneously injecting CT26 cells ${\bf\Omega}\cdot{\bf\Omega}^{2}\times10^{6}$ cells) into the backs of BALB/c mice.
When the surface diameter of tumors reached approximately $0.5~\mathrm{cm}$ , the tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into five groups $\left(\mathfrak{n}\ =\ 4\right)$ : (I) control, (II) AlHFs-PEG, (III) AlHFs-PEG $^+$ US, (IV) AlHFs-PEG $^+$ Light, and (V) AlHFs-PEG $^+$ Light $^+$ US. Mice were intratumorally injected with AlHFs-PEG ( $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $50~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ or PBS ${\bf\rho}_{\mathrm{100~\muL}})$ . After $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the tumors in group III-V were excited by US $(1.75\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $8~\mathrm{min}$ ), $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $8~\mathrm{min}$ ), or both US and light under the same conditions. After treatments, the weight of the mice and tumor volumes (length $\times\mathrm{\width}^{2}/2.$ ) were monitored over 16 days. The tumors and major organs were collected from the sacrificed mice, which were further cut into tumor slices for histological observation.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization of AlHFs-PEG and FeHFs-PEG
The AlHFs-PEG were prepared by synthesizing AlHFs through the self-assembly of HMME and $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions and then surfacemodifying them with amphiphilic PEGylated phospholipids (DSPE-PEG), as shown in Fig. 1. In the structure of AlHFs, HMME molecules originate from the acid hydrolysis of hemoglobin [31,32]. The carboxyl groups of HMME and Al(III) trimers can assemble into AlHFs, which is similar to the reported Al-based metal–organic frameworks [24]. Unlike heavy metals (such as Fe with an atomic number of 26 and $7.874\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ and an electron configuration of $4s^{2}3\mathsf{d}^{6}\mathsf{\cdot}$ , Al is a light metal with an atomic number of 13 and $2.7\:\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ and an electron configuration of $3s^{2}3\mathsf{p}^{1}$ [33]. As a result, the AlHFs are capable of much more efficient photosensitization and fluorescence in comparison to FeHFs, which can afford a high ${}^{1}0_{2}$ yield under irradiation with $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light and US.
The morphology of AlHFs was studied by using transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The TEM image (Fig. 2a) and size histogram (Fig. S1 in the Supporting Information) demonstrate that the AlHFs have an irregular morphology and an average size of $\sim185\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The high-resolution TEM image (Fig. 2b) and the corresponding fast-Fourier transform (FFT) pattern (Fig. 2c) confirm the amorphous structure of AlHFs due to the absence of lattice and diffraction spots, which are different from these crystalline Albased MOFs with clear lattices [24]. Moreover, to study the elemental composition, the AlHFs were examined by using energydispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The elemental maps (Fig. 2d) and EDS spectrum (Fig. 2e) demonstrate strong C, N, O, and Al elemental signals, verifying that the AlHFs are composed of ${\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{N}}/{\mathsf{O}}/{\mathsf{A l}}$ elements. In addition, the survey XPS spectrum (Fig. 2f) and Al 2p spectrum (Fig. 2g) present strong peaks of C 1 s, N 1 s, O 1 s, and Al 2p, in which Al 2p is positioned at $74.6\ \mathrm{{\eV}}$ , which is consistent with the binding energy of the $A1^{3+}$ state [25], indicating the existence of $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions within the structure of AlHFs. Based on the above results, amorphous and nanoscale AlHFs are synthesized by using HMME as the organic ligand and ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ ions as the metal nodes.
To investigate the effects of $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ -coordination on HMME molecules within the framework structure, free HMME and AlHFs were analyzed and compared by using UV–Vis spectroscopy, FT-IR spectroscopy, and fluorescence spectroscopy. Free HMME molecules in an ethanol solution show a strong and narrow Soret band at $398\ \mathrm{nm}$ as well as four Q bands at 498, 532, 568, and $622\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 3a). Compared to free HMME, the AlHFs also exhibit a Soret band but obviously broaden and present four redshifted Q bands to 502, 534, 570, and $624~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Of note, the redshift of peaks should be ascribed to $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking of molecules within the confined metal–organic nanostructure of AlHFs. In addition, the number of $\mathsf{Q}$ peaks remains four rather than being reduced to two or three, indicating the coordination between $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions and carboxyl groups [34]. In addition, the FT-IR spectra of HMME and AlHFs (Fig. 3b)
further confirm the coordination between ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions and the carboxyl groups of HMME. The FTIR spectrum of HMME demonstrates a distinct band at $1706~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which corresponds to carboxylic stretching, while the AlHFs show two carboxylate bands at 1654 and $1388~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [30]. For comparison, the FeHFs were also synthesized according to a previous paper [30]. The FeHFs in ethanol solution are red, similar to HMME solution, and they have an average size of $280~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. S2). The photoabsorption of FeHFs is similar to that of AlHFs, showing a redshift of $\mathsf{Q}$ bands in comparison to that of free HMME (Fig. S3). Subsequently, we examined the emission performance of HMME, AlHFs, and FeHFs at the same concentration of $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ in terms of HMME. Free HMME molecules have a prominent fluorescence band centered at $628~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with a stronger fluorescence intensity than that of the confined HMME in AlHFs and FeHFs (Fig. 3c). Among these samples, the fluorescence of FeHFs is significantly quenched $(\sim98\%)$ because $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions with unsaturated 3d orbitals can undergo the low-energy d-d transition to promote energy transfer [35]. It should be noted that, unlike heavy metal ions, $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions are light metal ions that are without 3d orbitals and do not facilitate energy transfer from activated HMME to ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions, thus leading to less fluorescence quenching $(\sim70\%)$ . Therefore, the AlHFs are constructed by the coordination of ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions and HMME, and they exhibit a higher fluorescence intensity in comparison to the as-synthesized FeHFs.
To improve colloidal stability, amphiphilic DSPE-PEG molecules containing two alkyl chains on one side and a PEG chain on the other side were used to surface-modify AlHFs to prepare AlHFsPEG, according to a previous paper [36]. The zeta potential (Fig. 3d) and dynamic light scattering (DLS, Fig. 3e) measurements of AlHFs and AlHFs-PEG were investigated. For AlHFs-PEG, the average zeta potential was determined to be $-13.0\ \mathrm{mV}$ , which is lower than that $\left(-3.4~\mathrm{mV}\right)$ of AlHFs due to the presence of negatively charged DSPE-PEG. Meanwhile, the hydrodynamic size increases from $220~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for AlHFs to $290~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for AlHFs-PEG. The decreased zeta potential and the increased hydrodynamic size suggest successful surface modification. Subsequently, the stability of AlHFs and AlHFs-PEG was studied in different biofluids, such as PBS, DMEM (Dulbecco’s modification of Eagle’s medium), and FBS (fetal bovine serum). After storage under ambient conditions, the AlHFs-PEG retained high dispersibility in these biofluids without any noticeable precipitation, whereas the AlHFs precipitated in PBS (Fig. 3f). Furthermore, we tested the time-dependent changes in the hydrodynamic diameters of AlHFs-PEG in these solutions, and the results indicated the absence of significant changes in diameters, indicating the high stability of AlHFs-PEG (Fig. S4). Thus, the DSPE-PEG coating endows AlHFs with good dispersibility and hydrophilicity.
## 3.2. Light- and US-driven $^1{\cal O}_{2}$ production
HMME molecules are excellent photosensitizers as well as sonosensitizers and can be effectively stimulated by light and US to produce $^10_{2}$ species through photodynamic and sonodynamic effects. To measure the photodynamic effect of AlHFs-PEG and FeHFs-PEG, the DPBF assay, which can be oxidized by $^10_{2}$ and show absorbance reduction, was used as the probe for evaluating the $^10_{2}$ level. When the DPBF solutions were separately treated with light $(100\mathrm{\mw}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ , $80\ s^{\cdot}$ ), AlHFs-PEG $(30~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ), or FeHFs-PEG $(30\upmu\mathrm{g}\:\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ , there was no change in absorbance (Fig. S5). In contrast, in terms of DPBF with AlHFs-PEG and light, the absorbance unceasingly decreases with increasing illumination time (Fig. 4a), indicating the oxidation of DPBF by the generated ${}^{1}0_{2}$ from lighttriggered AlHFs-PEG. Similarly, the absorbance of DPBF mediated with FeHFs-PEG is reduced with the illumination time (Fig. 4b), which confirms the photodynamic effect of FeHFs-PEG. The timedependent degradation rates of DPBF are summarized in (Fig. 4c). The degradation rate is determined to be $74.2\%$ for AlHFs-PEG, which is 3.6 times that $(20.6\%)$ of FeHFs. Therefore, AlHFs-PEG are capable of a higher $^10_{2}$ yield than FeHFs-PEG under light irradiation, showing a better photodynamic effect.
The sonodynamic effects of AlHFs-PEG and FeHFs-PEG were then studied. The solutions of AlHFs-PEG $(30~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ and DPBF were irradiated by US at $1.75\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ and the photoabsorption spectra were recorded every 3 min. The photoabsorption of DPBF solutions mediated with AlHFs-PEG and FeHFs-PEG decreased with increasing US exposure time, which verifies the generation of $^10_{2}$ (Fig. 4d, e). For the DPBF solution upon US treatment, the absorbance showed no change because US alone was unable to oxidize DPBF (Fig. S6). (Fig. 4f) presents the timedependent degradation rates of DPBF, and it confirms a higher oxidation rate $(43.9\%)$ of AlHFs-PEG than that $(15.8\%)$ of FeHFs-PEG. Thus, compared to FeHFs-PEG, AlHFs-PEG exhibit a higher ${}^{1}0_{2}$ yield due to the sonodynamic effect. The different photodynamic and sonodynamic effects between AlHFs-PEG and FeHFs-PEG can be attributed to the different energy transfer abilities of metal ions, as illustrated in (Fig. $\begin{array}{r}{4\mathbf{g}\dot{}\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\parallel\parallel\mathbf{}\parallel\parallel\parallel\parallel,}\end{}\mathrm\mathrm{}\mathrm{}\mathrm{~}\mathrm{~}\mathrm{~}\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~~\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm{~~}\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\mathrm\$ . Within the metal–organic structure of AlHFs and FeHFs, the HMME molecules interacted with $A1^{3+}$ ions and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions through covalent coordination. Under the stimulation of light or US, the HMME molecules are activated, whose energy can be converted by three main ways: (1) fluorescent emission, (2) energy transfer to the adjacent oxygen molecules to produce $^10_{2}$ , and (3) energy transfer to metal ions. The ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ion is a light metal ion without 3d orbitals that does not benefit the energy transfer from HMME to $A|^{3+}$ ions, while $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions with unsaturated 3d orbitals exhibit efficient energy transfer. Therefore, the fluorescence, photodynamic effect, and sonodynamic effect of FeHFs are effectively quenched in comparison to those of AlHFs.
## 3.3. Cytotoxicity and PDT-SDT therapies in vitro
Good biocompatibility is highly essential for the bioapplication of nanoagents. We used CT26 cells as the model of cancer cells, and they are one of the most malignant cancers. The cytotoxicity of AlHFs-PEG was evaluated in CT26 cells and HUVECs by first incubating cells with AlHFs-PEG at concentrations of $0{-}75~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for 12 or $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The cell viability was determined by the standard methyl thiazolyl tetrazolium (MTT) assay. After incubation for 12 or $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the viabilities of CT26 cells and HUVECs remained high (greater than $91.5\%$ , illustrating the high biocompatibility of AlHFs-PEG at the given concentrations (Fig. 5a and S7). Subsequently, the internalization of AlHFs-PEG by CT26 cells was studied by incubating AlHFs-PEG for different durations (1, 2, 4, and $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ ). As shown in (Fig. 5b), the cells show red fluorescence from AlHFs-PEG, which verifies the uptake of AlHFs-PEG by CT26 cells. It should be noted that with the increase in incubation time from 1 to $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the fluorescence intensity (Fig. 5c) of the cells increased, showing the time-dependent internalization of AlHFs-PEG by the cells. Therefore, AlHFs-PEG are capable of high biocompatibility and can be engulfed by cells.
After showing the internalization of AlHFs-PEG, we investigated the intracellular $^10_{2}$ generation by using the $^{2^{\prime},7^{\prime}}$ -dichlorofluores cein diacetate (DCFH-DA) staining assay. The cells were incubated with AlHFs-PEG at a concentration of $50~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ and then washed with PBS, followed by the addition of DCFH-DA for another $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ of incubation. For cells in the control group or separately treated with AlHFs-PEG, $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light $\mathrm{100\mW\cm^{-2}}$ , $5~\mathrm{min}$ ), or US $1.75\mathrm{W}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $5\mathrm{min}$ ), there was no obvious green fluorescence, indicating the absence of $^10_{2}$ within the cells (Fig. 5d and S8). When cells are treated with AlHFs-PEG and $660\ \mathrm{nm}$ light (100 mW $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $5~\mathrm{min}$ ), green fluorescence from the oxidized DCFH
DA appears within cells, which verifies the photodynamic effect of AlHFs-PEG. Similarly, green fluorescence can also be observed when the AlHFs-PEG incubated cells are illuminated by the US at 1.75 W $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , thus confirming the sonodynamic effect of AlHFs-PEG within cells. Moreover, the AlHFs-PEG-incubated cells were exposed to both light and US, and they exhibited the strongest green fluorescence among the other cells (Fig. 5e). The above results confirm that AlHFs-PEG within cells can generate ${}^{1}0_{2}$ upon irradiation with $660\ \mathrm{nm}$ light and US, affording photodynamic and sonodynamic effects.
Furthermore, the treated cells were stained with Calcein-AM (green fluorescent for living cells) and PI (red fluorescent for dead cells). After being separately treated with US for 8 min, $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light for $8~\mathrm{min}$ , or AlHFs-PEG $(50\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ , the cells showed green fluorescence, suggesting the safety of US, $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light, or AlHFsPEG (Fig. 6d and S9). For cells in the AlHFs-PEG $^+$ Light group and AlHFs-PEG $+{\mathrm{US}}$ group, some of them exhibit red fluorescence, which indicates that some of the cells are destroyed due to the cytotoxicity of $^10_{2}$ generated from AlHFs-PEG under irradiation with light and US. In the case of cells in the AlHFs-PEG $^+$ Light $^+$ US group, there was only red fluorescence, which confirms the high efficiency of the combined PDT-SDT therapy. Therefore, AlHFs-PEG can serve as an excellent photosensitizer and sonosensitizer, exerting the combined PDT-SDT for cancer cells.
## 3.4. SDT-PDT therapy of tumors
Given the combined PDT-SDT in vitro, we then investigated the therapeutic effect of AlHFs-PEG in vivo. BALB/c mice were subcutaneously injected with CT26 cells to establish the tumor model, and then the tumors received different treatments. Finally, the mice were sacrificed (Fig. 7a). Before tumor treatment, blood compatibility was studied, which is fundamentally important for the feasible administration of AlHFs-PEG in vivo. After incubation with AlHFs-PEG at concentrations up to $120\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , no obvious hemolysis of red blood cells was observed, indicating that AlHFs-PEG have good blood compatibility (Fig. S10). Moreover, we investigated the blood biochemistry of healthy mice after intravenous injection with AlHFs-PEG $\zeta\ \mathrm{mg\kg^{-1}}$ ). Compared to the mice in the control group, the mice treated with AlHFs-PEG exhibited no obvious difference in liver function markers (aspartate aminotransferase and alanine aminotransferase) or kidney function markers (urea nitrogen and albumin/globin ratio), indicating that AlHFs-PEG did not cause any hepatic or kidney disorders in mice (Table S2). Thus, the above results confirm the high biosafety of AlHFs-PEG.
The CT26 tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into five groups $\mathrm{\dot{n}}=4$ per group): I) control group, II) AlHFs-PEG group, III) AlHFs-PEG $+{\mathrm{~US~}}$ group, IV) AlHFs-PEG $^+$ Light group, and V) AlHFs$\mathsf{P E G}+\mathsf{U S}+$ Light group. The mice in the control group were intratumorally injected with $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ PBS, and the mice in groups II-V were intratumorally injected with AlHFs-PEG in PBS ( $100\ \upmu\mathrm{L},$ ， $50\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ). After $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ , the tumor area was exposed to US $(1.75~\mathrm{W~cm}^{-2}$ , $8~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) for mice in group III, light $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ , $8\mathrm{min}^{\cdot}$ ) for mice in group IV, or both US and light for mice in Group V. After treatment, there was no loss in body weight, illustrating no systemic toxicity for all mice (Fig. 7b). In addition, the growth rates of tumors in Groups I and II were rapid, and the volume on the 16th day was 11 times that of their initial volume (Fig. 7c). In contrast, compared to their initial volume, the tumor volume was increased by 7.4 times in group III and by 4.1 times in group IV, confirming the therapeutic effect of AlHFs-PEG upon US or light. Moreover, the tumors in Group V had the slowest growth rate among the other groups, and the growth rate increased by 2.8 times, indicating the high therapeutic effect of the combined PDT-SDT by AlHFsPEG. (Fig. 7d) shows the typical photographs of mice with tumor growth profiles on the 0, 8th, and 16th days after therapy. Vividly, the tumor growth rate in Groups I and II was faster than that in groups III-V. Therefore, the combined PDT-SDT of AlHFs-PEG has a higher tumor therapeutic efficacy than PDT or SDT alone.
Furthermore, mice were sacrificed on the 16th day to harvest tumors and organs for further histological examination. For the hematoxylin and eosin (H&E)-stained slices of tumors in Groups I and II, the tumor cells had complete cell morphology with clear cell nuclei and cytoplasm (Fig. 7e), which indicates the absence of a therapeutic effect of AlHFs-PEG alone. In contrast, the tumor cells in groups III-V exhibited abnormal cell morphology and condensed nuclei, and tumor cells in Group V suffered from serious damage, indicating a high therapeutic effect of AlHFs-PEG upon US and light. In addition, no obvious histological difference was found in slices of major organs between Group I and Group V (Fig. 8), showing the high biocompatibility of AlHFs-PEG. These results suggest that AlHFs-PEG have high therapeutic efficacy on tumor cells and good biocompatibility.
## 4. Conclusions
Numerous nanoscale metal–organic frameworks consisting of heavy metal ions (such as $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+},\bar{\mathsf{C u}}^{2+},\mathsf{M n}^{3+})$ have been reported for cancer treatments, but they usually suffer from serious quenching in fluorescence and photo-sono sensitization [6,20,37]. To solve these problems, we synthesized nanoscale hematoporphrin-based frameworks $\left(\sim185\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ with ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions, one of the light metal ions, as metal nodes through covalent coordination, and they have high hydrophilicity and dispersibility after the surface modification of DSPE-PEG molecules. The AlHFs-PEG exhibit high fluorescence intensity and can produce 3.6-fold or 2.8-fold more $^10_{2}$ species than the FeHFs-PEG upon illumination with $660\ \mathrm{nm}$ light or ultrasound, respectively. Given the high biosafety and enhanced photosono sensitization, AlHFs-PEG are intratumorally injected into tumors whose growth rates are inhibited upon irradiation with light or ultrasound due to their photodynamic and sonodynamic effects. Importantly, the combined PDT-SDT of AlHFs-PEG has a higher tumor therapeutic efficacy than PDT or SDT alone. Therefore, the present work reports AlHFs-PEG as a new type of photosono sensitizer and provides some insight for developing nanoscale frameworks with light metal ions as metal nodes. In our future work, we will conjugate AlHFs-PEG with tumor-targeting molecules, study the therapeutic effect on an orthotopic transplanted tumor model or a metastatic tumor model, and investigate the tumor microenvironment response and metabolism of AlHFs-PEG.
